  <h2>How does the Weibo nine-grid crop images?</h2>

  <p>It shows square tiles. How it gets from your image to a square is the part nobody current describes.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Published sources agree that Weibo shows two to nine images as square tiles. Only one describes the mechanism, a center crop that keeps the middle of each image, and it was written about eight years ago. Weibo's own help center now mentions a focus point that can be set on most images, but does not say what it controls. No source in either language describes a grid that pads rather than crops.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: 4 independent published references and 1 Weibo help-center page dated 1 April 2026, Chinese and English, collected 10 September 2026 and grouped by the mechanism each describes. Consensus on square tiles, contested on the crop.</p>

  <p>An eight-year-old description of a center crop and an undefined focus point are not the same instruction, and nothing public says which one runs now. So the practical answer is to upload the shape that makes the question irrelevant.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Whichever account of the Weibo image grid is right, a square image comes through unchanged: a center crop takes nothing from it, a focus point has nothing to move, and no padding is needed. For a post that runs past nine images, keep the lower right corner of the ninth tile clear, where the hidden-image count sits.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: derived 10 September 2026 from the published grid accounts and the 2026 help-center focus-point statement. A safe intersection, not a platform rule.</p>

  <p>Square uploads for the grid, and nothing important in the ninth tile's lower right corner. Both are derivations, and both cost nothing.</p>

  <h2>What are the Weibo video specs?</h2>

  <p>Weibo's help center gives the upload limits and the cover ratios. It does not give a maximum duration.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Weibo's video upload FAQ, last updated on 1 July 2024, states that a video uploaded from a PC may be up to 15G and one uploaded from a phone up to 4G, and that the phone will not accept a video shorter than three seconds. Vertical videos take a 3:4 cover and cannot take a horizontal one. Horizontal videos take a 16:9 or 4:3 cover. The page does not state a maximum duration.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: kefu.weibo.com video upload and publishing FAQ, page dated 1 July 2024, fetched 10 September 2026, Chinese-language. Primary reading, scoped to that FAQ.</p>

  <p>Read the cover rule carefully. Those ratios describe the cover image, not the video frame. A vertical video takes a 3:4 cover. That says nothing about the ratio of the video itself, and the FAQ publishes no frame ratio at all.</p>

